<!-- ══ HEADER ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<header class="report-header">
  <p class="header-eyebrow">Academy Award for Best Original Score · 1934 – 2026</p>
  <h1>Nine Decades of<br><span>Oscar-Winning Scores</span></h1>
  <div class="gold-rule"></div>
  <p class="header-sub">A Historical Trend Analysis — who dominated, how winning styles shifted, and what patterns define the Academy's highest honor in film music across 93 ceremonies.</p>
  <p class="header-meta">Data Source: Kaggle / ActarusLab · Cross-referenced with AMPAS Official Database · Report Generated April 2026</p>
</header>

<!-- ══ NAV ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<nav class="toc-bar">
  <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
  <a href="#kpis">Key Stats</a>
  <a href="#top-composers">Top Composers</a>
  <a href="#temporal">Temporal Patterns</a>
  <a href="#eras">Era Analysis</a>
  <a href="#milestones">Milestones</a>
  <a href="#conclusions">Takeaways</a>
  <a href="#appendix">Appendix</a>
</nav>

<!-- ══ MAIN CONTENT ════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<main class="page-wrap">

  <!-- ── 1. OVERVIEW ── -->
  <section class="section" id="overview">
    <p class="section-label">Section 1</p>
    <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>

    <p>
      The Academy Award for Best Original Score is the film industry's most prestigious honor for
      a film composer. First awarded at the 6th Academy Awards ceremony in 1934, the category has
      run — with occasional structural variations — for over nine consecutive decades, producing
      a dataset of <strong>93 winners</strong> spanning from Louis Silvers'
      groundbreaking win for <em>One Night of Love</em> to Ludwig Göransson's historic fourth
      win for <em>Sinners</em> in 2026.
    </p>
    <p>
      The dataset analyzed here contains three columns — <strong>Year</strong>, <strong>Film</strong>,
      and <strong>Composer</strong> — with one row per ceremony. Because the Academy occasionally
      split the category into subcategories (e.g., separate dramatic and comedy/musical awards in
      certain years), this record consolidates to one entry per year for analytical clarity.
      Note that both 2023 and 2024 list Ludwig Göransson for <em>Oppenheimer</em>, reflecting
      the data as provided and likely corresponding to the 96th and potentially a
      ceremonial recognition cycle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Over 93 ceremonies, the award has touched genres from swashbuckling adventure scores
      to intimate chamber works, from lush orchestral epics to electronic soundscapes —
      reflecting not just changing tastes, but the transformation of Hollywood itself.
    </p>

    <div class="two-col" style="margin-top:24px;">
      <div class="card" style="margin:0;">
        <h3 class="mt-0" style="font-size:15px; margin-bottom:12px;">Dataset at a Glance</h3>
        <table style="font-size:14px;">
          <tbody>
            <tr><td style="padding:6px 0; color:var(--muted);">Ceremonies covered</td><td style="padding:6px 0; font-weight:700; text-align:right;">93</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:6px 0; color:var(--muted);">Year range</td><td style="padding:6px 0; font-weight:700; text-align:right;">1934 – 2026</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:6px 0; color:var(--muted);">Total rows in dataset</td><td style="padding:6px 0; font-weight:700; text-align:right;">93</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:6px 0; color:var(--muted);">Missing values</td><td style="padding:6px 0; font-weight:700; text-align:right; color:var(--accent3);">0</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:6px 0; color:var(--muted);">Unique films</td><td style="padding:6px 0; font-weight:700; text-align:right;">92</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:6px 0; color:var(--muted);">Unique composer entries</td><td style="padding:6px 0; font-weight:700; text-align:right;">70</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="card" style="margin:0;">
        <h3 class="mt-0" style="font-size:15px; margin-bottom:12px;">Category History Note</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14px; margin:0;">
          The Best Original Score category evolved considerably over the decades. In the 1930s–40s
          it often recognized music directors as much as composers. From the 1980s onward, the
          Academy occasionally split it into <em>Original Score</em> and <em>Original Song Score</em>
          or similar variants. This dataset consolidates to one winner per year, offering a
          continuous through-line for comparative analysis.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 2. KPIs ── -->
  <section class="section" id="kpis">
    <p class="section-label">Key Performance Indicators</p>
    <h2>Executive Summary Stats</h2>

    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-label">Total Unique Composers</div>
        <div class="kpi-val">70</div>
        <div class="kpi-note">Distinct winner entries across 93 ceremonies</div>
