          Three composers share the outright record of four wins. <strong>John Williams</strong>
          accumulated his wins across the 1970s–90s with landmark blockbusters (<em>Jaws</em>,
          <em>Star Wars</em>, <em>E.T.</em>, <em>Schindler's List</em>). <strong>John Barry</strong>
          spread his four wins across a remarkable 24-year arc from 1966–1990, demonstrating an
          unusual ability to stay Academy-relevant across three different era shifts.
          <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong> has been the most explosive accumulator: four wins
          in just eight years (2018–2026), a pace that no prior composer has matched.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card" style="margin:0;">
        <h4 style="font-size:14px; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; color: var(--muted); letter-spacing:1px; text-transform:uppercase; margin-bottom:12px;">The Consecutive-Win Anomaly</h4>
        <p style="font-size:14px;">
          <strong>Alan Menken</strong> won three times in four years (1989, 1991, 1992), riding
          Disney's Animation Renaissance. <strong>Franz Waxman</strong> won back-to-back in 1950
          and 1951 (<em>Sunset Boulevard</em> and <em>A Place in the Sun</em>) — the only instance
          of consecutive solo wins until Gustavo Santaolalla in 2005–2006. <strong>Göransson</strong>
          then won in 2023 and 2024 for the same film (<em>Oppenheimer</em>), a unique circumstance
          in the dataset.
        </p>
