  <!-- ── 5. ERA ANALYSIS ── -->
  <section class="section" id="eras">
    <p class="section-label">Section 4</p>
    <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>

    <p>
      While the dataset contains only Year, Film, and Composer, the winning composers and films
      map clearly onto four broadly recognized eras of Hollywood film music. These eras reflect
      both aesthetic shifts and changes in who the Academy chose to honor.
    </p>

    <!-- Golden Age -->
    <div class="card">
      <div style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:12px; margin-bottom:16px;">
        <span class="era-tag era-golden">Golden Age</span>
        <strong>1934 – ~1960 · 27 Ceremonies</strong>
      </div>
      <p>
        The Golden Age of Hollywood scoring was defined by massive studio orchestras, European-trained
        Romantic composers, and the vocabulary of late 19th-century concert music applied to cinema.
        The Oscar winners of this era — <strong>Max Steiner</strong>, <strong>Erich Wolfgang Korngold</strong>,
        <strong>Dimitri Tiomkin</strong>, <strong>Alfred Newman</strong>, <strong>Miklos Rozsa</strong>,
        and <strong>Franz Waxman</strong> — were largely émigré European composers who brought classical
        training and lush orchestration to the Hollywood sound stage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Concentration was high: repeat wins were common, and the award largely circulated among a
        small circle of studio composers. The 1950s alone saw 5 repeat wins — the highest concentration
        of any Golden Age decade. Tiomkin's three wins in six years (1952, 1954, 1958) and Waxman's
        consecutive wins are this era's defining statistical feature.
      </p>
      <p>
        Notable outliers: <strong>Aaron Copland</strong>'s win for <em>The Heiress</em> (1949) and
        <strong>Brian Easdale</strong>'s win for <em>The Red Shoes</em> (1948) — a rare British
        win — hint at the era's occasional willingness to reward unconventional voices.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Silver Age -->
    <div class="card">
      <div style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:12px; margin-bottom:16px;">
        <span class="era-tag era-silver">Silver Age</span>
        <strong>~1960 – ~1980 · 21 Ceremonies</strong>
      </div>
      <p>
        The Silver Age brought the New Hollywood wave and a parallel revolution in film music.
        European film composers — especially French and British — began dominating the Oscar stage.
        <strong>Maurice Jarre</strong> (<em>Lawrence of Arabia</em>, <em>Doctor Zhivago</em>),
        <strong>John Barry</strong> (<em>Born Free</em>, <em>The Lion in Winter</em>),
        <strong>Francis Lai</strong>, and <strong>Michel Legrand</strong> brought a cooler,
        more impressionistic language to the award.
      </p>
      <p>
        The late 1970s marked a pivotal inflection: <strong>John Williams</strong>' two wins
        for <em>Jaws</em> and <em>Star Wars</em> (1975, 1977) signaled a return to heroic,
        thematic orchestral writing — just as electronic scoring (Giorgio Moroder, 1978) appeared
        for the first time. The Silver Age thus contained both the Academy's most adventurous
        moment and the seed of the coming blockbuster era.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Nino Rota <em>Godfather</em> wins (1972 solo, 1974 shared with Carmine Coppola)
        stand as this era's most iconic film-score pairing, cementing the relationship between
        auteur cinema and distinctive, memorable musical identity.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Modern Era -->
    <div class="card">
      <div style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:12px; margin-bottom:16px;">
        <span class="era-tag era-modern">Modern Era</span>
        <strong>~1980 – ~2000 · 21 Ceremonies</strong>
      </div>
      <p>
        The Modern Era is defined by two competing forces: the rise of the blockbuster score
        (Williams' continued presence, Jerry Goldsmith, John Barry's swansong wins) and the
        increasing recognition of musical outliers. <strong>Vangelis</strong>' electronic
        score for <em>Chariots of Fire</em> (1981) and <strong>Herbie Hancock</strong>'s
        jazz-rooted win for <em>Round Midnight</em> (1986) represent the Academy's
        willingness to honor compositional approaches outside the symphonic mainstream.
      </p>
      <p>
        The era's most defining streak was <strong>Alan Menken</strong>'s three wins in
        four years (1989, 1991, 1992), driven by Disney's animated renaissance.
        <em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, and <em>Aladdin</em>
        represent the only instance of a single composer winning three times within a four-year window.
      </p>
      <p>
        The latter part of the Modern Era (1994–1999) then delivered its most diverse
        five-year run: Hans Zimmer, Luis Bacalov, Gabriel Yared, James Horner, Nicola Piovani,
        John Corigliano — no repeats, maximum variety, and the first serious wave of
        non-American, non-European winners on the horizon.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Contemporary -->
    <div class="card">
      <div style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:12px; margin-bottom:16px;">
        <span class="era-tag era-contemp">Contemporary Era</span>
        <strong>~2000 – 2026 · 27 Ceremonies</strong>
      </div>
      <p>
        The Contemporary Era is the most globally diverse in the award's history. Between 2000
        and 2020, winners came from Sweden (Göransson), Iceland (Guðnadóttir), China (Tan Dun),
        India (A.R. Rahman), Argentina (Santaolalla), Italy (Morricone), Canada (Shore), and
        beyond — reflecting Hollywood's globalization and the Academy's shifting demographics.
      </p>
      <p>
        Electronic and hybrid scores have become normalized. <strong>Trent Reznor &amp; Atticus Ross</strong>
        won for <em>The Social Network</em> (2010) and returned (with Jon Batiste) for <em>Soul</em>
        (2020), bridging art-rock production sensibilities and Academy taste. <strong>A.R. Rahman</strong>'s
        win for <em>Slumdog Millionaire</em> (2008) remains the most globally recognized contemporary win.
      </p>
      <p>
        The era closes — to date — with <strong>Ludwig Göransson</strong>'s extraordinary run:
        four wins in eight years (2018–2026) including three across a four-year span (2023–2026).
        Göransson's versatility — from the Afrofuturist orchestration of <em>Black Panther</em>
        to the atomic tension of <em>Oppenheimer</em> to the blues-infused <em>Sinners</em> —
        defines the Contemporary Era's defining composer just as Williams defined the Modern.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 6. MILESTONES ── -->
  <section class="section" id="milestones">
    <p class="section-label">Section 5</p>
    <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>

    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1934</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>First winner:</strong> Louis Silvers for <em>One Night of Love</em> — the Academy inaugurates
          the Best Original Score category at the 6th Awards ceremony.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1935</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>First repeat winner begins:</strong> Max Steiner wins for <em>The Informer</em>; he will
          go on to win again in 1943 and 1945, establishing the template for career dominance.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1939</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>First ensemble/shared win:</strong> Richard Hageman, W. Franke Harling, John Leipold,
          and Leo Shuken shared the award for <em>Stagecoach</em> — a four-way split that remained
          one of the largest composer groups ever credited.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1950–1951</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>First consecutive wins by a solo composer:</strong> Franz Waxman won back-to-back
          for <em>Sunset Boulevard</em> and <em>A Place in the Sun</em> — the only such occurrence
          until Santaolalla (2005–06) and Göransson (2023–24).
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1966–1990</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>Longest span of wins by one composer:</strong> John Barry's four wins span 24 years —
          the widest career window of any repeat winner in the dataset.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1978</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>First electronic score to win:</strong> Giorgio Moroder's synthesizer-driven score
          for <em>Midnight Express</em> broke the orchestral dominance and opened the door
          for non-traditional scoring approaches.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1981</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>Vangelis wins for <em>Chariots of Fire</em>:</strong> A landmark electronic score win
          that demonstrated the Academy's willingness to honor synthesizer-based composition
          at the height of its relevance.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1986</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>Jazz's only solo Oscar:</strong> Herbie Hancock's win for <em>Round Midnight</em>
          remains the single instance of a jazz composer taking the Best Original Score prize
          as a solo winner.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1989–1992</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>Alan Menken's concentration record:</strong> Three wins in four years for Disney
          animated features — the tightest multi-win cluster in the award's history for any
          solo non-consecutive years.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">1994</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>Hans Zimmer's first win</strong> for <em>The Lion King</em>. He would wait
          27 years for his second — for <em>Dune</em> in 2021 — the longest gap between wins
          for any two-time winner in the dataset.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">2015</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>Ennio Morricone wins at 87</strong> for <em>The Hateful Eight</em> — his first
          competitive Oscar after five previous nominations and an honorary award in 2007.
          Widely considered one of the most celebrated late-career wins in Academy history.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <div class="timeline-year">2018–2026</div>
        <div class="timeline-text">
          <strong>Ludwig Göransson's historic run:</strong> Four wins in eight years place him
          alongside Williams and Barry at the all-time record — but at a pace (one win per
          two years) that exceeds both predecessors. His win for <em>Sinners</em> in 2026
          is the most recent entry in the dataset.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 7. CONCLUSIONS ── -->
  <section class="section" id="conclusions">
    <p class="section-label">Section 6</p>
    <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>

    <ul class="takeaway-list">
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">1</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <strong>The award concentrates in bursts, then diversifies.</strong> The 1950s and 2020s
          represent twin peaks of concentration (5 and 5 repeat wins per decade respectively),
          while the 1980s and 2010s represent peaks of diversity (10 and 9 unique entries).
          This oscillation between concentration and dispersal is the dominant structural
          pattern across nine decades — and suggests the 2030s may deliver a new wave of
          fresh winners to counterbalance Göransson's dominance.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">2</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <strong>Four wins is the ceiling — held by three composers across different eras.</strong>
          John Williams (1975–1993), John Barry (1966–1990), and Ludwig Göransson (2018–2026)
          each reached exactly four wins. No composer has surpassed this mark. Göransson's
          ongoing career makes him the most likely candidate to set a new record in the coming decade.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">3</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <strong>Globalization of the award accelerated sharply after 2000.</strong> The
          2000s and 2010s brought winners from Asia, South America, Iceland, and beyond —
          reflecting both Hollywood's expanding production partnerships and the Academy's
          diversifying membership. This trend appears durable and structural rather than
          episodic.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">4</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <strong>Electronic and hybrid scores are now fully Academy-normalized.</strong>
          From Moroder (1978) through Reznor/Ross (2010, 2020) to Göransson's genre-defying
          blends, the orchestral monopoly has definitively ended. The 2020s have seen winning
          scores that defy easy genre classification — a trend that is likely to accelerate
          as AI-assisted composition and new sonic technologies enter the film music ecosystem.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">5</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">
          <strong>Repeat winners dominate headlines but first-timers remain the statistical norm.</strong>
          With a 28% repeat-winner rate, 72% of ceremonies have been won by composers winning
          for the first time. The award remains genuinely open — the 70-entry unique-composer
          count across 93 ceremonies reflects this. The story of the Oscar for Best Original
          Score is not just about its dynasties; it is equally about the steady stream of
          singular careers that touched the award once and shaped film music history
          on their own terms.
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

